WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:08.650
Bridget: Hi, I'm Bridget Hillyer.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.100
I'm one of the organizers at Strange Loop.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.350
In this series we've been talking to previous
speakers.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:18.670
But now that we have a program for this year
we're talking to some feature speakers, Strange

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:20.490
Loop is on in person this year.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:26.580
It's September 30th through October 2nd, 2021
in St. Louis, Missouri USA.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.749
You can find out more information and get
tickets at the website.

00:00:31.749 --> 00:00:32.749
https://thestrangeloop.com.

00:00:32.749 --> 00:00:34.410
Also this year, we have a virtual option.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:38.880
So if you aren't able to make it in person,
you can still be part of the conference.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.600
So you can also find out information about
that on the website.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.590
Today I'm delighted to have Tejas Chopra right
here who will be speaking this year in October.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:52.340
And the name of his talk is Carbon Footprint
Aware Software Development.

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:55.530
Thanks for being with me today, it will be
nice to talk to you.

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.579
Tejas: Yes, same here, Bridget.

00:00:57.579 --> 00:00:59.520
And thank you everyone for listening again.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.590
And I'm excited to talk about carbon footprint
and software.

00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:07.140
Bridget: So today I'd just like to talk about
some background and some things around carbon

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.100
footprint aware software development.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:13.410
So my first question, when I saw your talk
and that the abstract.

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:19.230
What I was first curious about is what led
you to be interested in carbon footprint aware

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.470
software development?

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:24.441
Was it something you would worked on, you
know, some knowledge you had something in

00:01:24.441 --> 00:01:25.441
your background.

00:01:25.441 --> 00:01:26.510
Tejas: Yeah, that's that's a great question.

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:29.810
I think it's a, it's a series of steps that
led me to where I am.

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:34.800
The first thing I would say is that I got
interested in Bitcoin in the year 2013, 2014,

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:35.950
just when it was very nascent.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:40.980
And one of the things with Bitcoin was that,
oh, it's an energy gobbler.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.510
So I started reading more about it, more about
proof of work.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:49.110
And I saw that, yes, in fact, Bitcoin actually
consumes a lot of energy today.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:52.250
And that actually led me to take a step back.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:57.520
And look at other areas of my life, where,
you know, I'm seeing carbon footprint increase

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.820
in carbon footprint and there are two ways.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:04.420
Really one thing is as a human being, you
use a lot of services.

00:02:04.420 --> 00:02:08.020
You use your smartphone, you use the internet,
you use a car.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:12.690
So those are things that you can definitely
choose to buy stuff appropriately.

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:17.650
And the other thing is, as a software developer,
you work on software.

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:20.010
And it's when I tried to research what into
it.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:26.299
I realized that 4% of the total carbon footprint
on this planet is contributed by software.

00:02:26.299 --> 00:02:32.030
So I know that it is not 4% seems very minuscule
compared to, you know, 20%, 30% that we have

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:36.140
out there with cars, with with you know, agriculture
and other things.

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:41.849
So, but if you can make a dent in that 4%,
because that is completely in your control.

00:02:41.849 --> 00:02:45.260
That would really go a long way in trying
to make this planet better.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:47.879
So that was what was the motivation that I
had.

00:02:47.879 --> 00:02:50.670
And then I connected it back to what I had
learned in school.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.319
So I'm from Carnegie Mellon university.

00:02:52.319 --> 00:02:58.659
In school, we had lectures around, you know,
how can you write more efficient software?

00:02:58.659 --> 00:03:01.080
And then I tried to club these two thoughts
together.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:04.890
Can we write software that is efficient?

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:05.980
Better for the environment.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.620
And there are simple techniques as software
engineers that you can employ in your day-to-day

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:10.620
life.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.569
So that's where, you know, these dots connected
and that's how I got interested into it.

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:18.189
And I've been using it in my work since that
point on, and I am a strong advocate of, you

00:03:18.189 --> 00:03:23.209
know, having making a change in the environment
because we should always leave back the world

00:03:23.209 --> 00:03:24.859
better than what we got it.

00:03:24.859 --> 00:03:26.160
So that's the motivation.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:31.290
Bridget: So Bitcoin, I'm not surprised to
hear that, that, that was an inspiration there.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:36.499
I think when people think about this topic,
that's the first thing they think of because

00:03:36.499 --> 00:03:38.389
there's a lot of talk about it.

00:03:38.389 --> 00:03:44.150
So based on what you've learned, what do you
think the hope is for Bitcoin?

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:47.999
For what could be done there to reduce the
carbon footprint?

00:03:47.999 --> 00:03:48.999
Tejas: Sure.

00:03:48.999 --> 00:03:49.999
Sure.

00:03:49.999 --> 00:03:54.469
So one thing is that there is also a misnomer
out there that Bitcoin energy is like Bitcoin

00:03:54.469 --> 00:03:56.249
is really bad for the environment.

00:03:56.249 --> 00:04:00.819
It is bad because it uses a lot of energy,
but if you compare it to the industries that

00:04:00.819 --> 00:04:06.700
is trying to disrupt such as traditional banking
or gold, the energy usage in those areas or

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:09.609
those industries is still much higher than
Bitcoin.

00:04:09.609 --> 00:04:14.599
And if you look at what the aim of Bitcoin
is, it is to actually give financial independence

00:04:14.599 --> 00:04:18.880
to people that are not really reached out
by traditional banking.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.739
So it really will make people's lives better.

00:04:21.739 --> 00:04:27.009
So in that sense, I still feel that, you know,
the energy usage in Bitcoin is a problem,

00:04:27.009 --> 00:04:31.790
but there are far bigger problems as well
out there for, for reducing the energy consumption.

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:32.970
That's the first point.

00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:37.030
The second point is that yes, proof of work
is bad for the environment.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:42.150
So it is, unfortunately the way Bitcoin is
designed, it will keep using proof of work.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:45.700
There are alternatives such as Ethereum, which
use proof of stake.

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:50.500
And with Ethereum 2.0 the amount of energy
will go down by 99.9%.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.620
When 2.0 just comes out.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:55.620
So that's the other thing.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:05:00.250
I have seen that recently, Bitcoin proof of
work algorithms like the mining farms and

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:04.810
oil, they are using renewable energy sources
at locations where previously we were not

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:06.980
able to tap into them.

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:10.960
So a very good example is El Salvador, which
is introducing Bitcoin.

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:16.510
They actually want to mine it using the volcano
that they have so energy from the volcano.

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:20.210
So I think these are interesting ideas that
people are coming up with.

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:25.590
To circumvent the problems and these ideas
can also be applied in other industries.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:27.190
So it's not really a lose, lose.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:31.860
I still feel that there is something to gain
from our experience with Bitcoin and with

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:36.530
increase in usability, increase in scalability
things will get better.

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:37.680
That's what I think.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:38.680
Yeah.

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:42.590
Bridget: It's nice to talk to somebody who
has some real perspective on, on it and has

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:47.800
thought through the problems as opposed to
just "Bitcoin is bad for the environment.

00:05:47.800 --> 00:05:48.800
What do we do?"

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:54.550
So you work at Netflix and it's I think anybody
would hear that and hear you talking about

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:58.590
a carbon footprint of where software development
would then have the question, right?

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:00.440
So what is Netflix doing?

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:07.020
And, and if when you're thinking about things
like that, like Bitcoin, Netflix has the potential

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:14.100
to have a lot of impact if they were to employ
things that improved their carbon footprint.

00:06:14.100 --> 00:06:20.220
So how has that affected how you look at how
software development works at Netflix.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:25.660
Tejas: And this is a very pertinent question
because especially now that we are all in

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:29.010
remote work, in some ways the streaming industry
has boomed.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:33.740
We have so many streaming providers and Netflix
has 200 million subscribers, 200 million plus

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:34.740
subscribers.

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:39.890
So any dent that we can make in this space
goes a long way to improve the environment.

00:06:39.890 --> 00:06:44.560
And it's also a very pertinent time actually,
because recently the carbon trust group did

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:45.560
a study.

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:50.190
It's an independent group and they claimed
in the study that an hour of streaming a Netflix

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:56.381
movie in the UK the amount of energy that
it uses is equivalent to boiling the kettle

00:06:56.381 --> 00:07:00.940
for six minutes or like popping four bags
of popcorn in a microwave.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:06.250
So in that sense this has an, just to give
you numbers there it's 55 grams of carbon

00:07:06.250 --> 00:07:11.590
dioxide released in the air by streaming a
Netflix movie for an hour.

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:17.430
50% of it is contributed by the last mile,
which is the user's phone or users device.

00:07:17.430 --> 00:07:20.250
Which really you cannot control beyond an
extent.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:25.580
50% of it is then the network infrastructure,
the data centers, the REST request calls,

00:07:25.580 --> 00:07:27.290
all of those taking up energy.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:31.310
And Netflix has gotten really better at it
because at the start of the year it was around

00:07:31.310 --> 00:07:35.810
a hundred grams of carbon dioxide for an hour,
at least by some study.

00:07:35.810 --> 00:07:37.620
And now it is down to 55 grams.

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.100
And it really has gotten better over the years.

00:07:40.100 --> 00:07:44.290
So we are aiming to be greenhouse emission
neutral.

00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:46.760
I think that's the word by 2022.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:52.840
So in that sense, we we are as a company,
very invested in you know, making the environment

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:53.840
better.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:55.850
And Netflix again has two parts to it.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:59.630
There is a physical production where you actually
produce movies and then there is a streaming

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:00.680
part of it.

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:05.170
The physical production is the one that generates
a lot more carbon dioxide or has a bigger

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:07.860
energy footprint than the streaming part.

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:12.450
So to offset that we would then buy credits
carbon credits and that's how we'll get carbon

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:13.720
neutral.

00:08:13.720 --> 00:08:17.090
So that's what Netflix is doing on that front.

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:21.200
And I personally work on the storage infrastructure
side.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:26.690
So I work as a part of a team that builds
storage solutions for Netflix Studios and

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.240
streaming platforms.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.640
And we have petabytes to exabytes of storage
that we deal with at Netflix.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:38.130
So what we try to do in our day-to-day work
is, you know, write efficient code that reduces

00:08:38.130 --> 00:08:42.589
the footprint of storage in the cloud which
effectively then boils down to how much energy

00:08:42.589 --> 00:08:44.070
gets used in the cloud.

00:08:44.070 --> 00:08:47.870
One thing is that you pay for it and you have
money for it and that's fine.

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:52.009
Like Netflix has money, but the other thing
is, is this good for the environment?

00:08:52.009 --> 00:08:55.520
And that's the bigger motivation for us as
engineers here.

00:08:55.520 --> 00:09:00.630
Also in my day-to-day work, I'm translating
all these ideas into software techniques,

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:04.329
for example, a simple thing like removing
dead code right.

00:09:04.329 --> 00:09:08.730
You may think that fine if you don't remove
it, it's just lying there, but it actually

00:09:08.730 --> 00:09:14.029
takes up energy cycles to, you know, process
it and not actually execute that dead code.

00:09:14.029 --> 00:09:18.269
So in some ways these little steps having
event driven architectures versus polling

00:09:18.269 --> 00:09:23.800
based architectures, they are things that
reduce your CPU usage and then translate into

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:26.050
a better experience for the environment.

00:09:26.050 --> 00:09:29.639
So these are things that, you know on top
of my head that I can think of that we are

00:09:29.639 --> 00:09:30.860
doing as engineers.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:36.160
Bridget: Well, that's great to hear that Netflix
and especially with the, those aggressive

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:40.190
targets, like by 2022, that's that's great
to hear that's comforting.

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:44.310
So some of the things you've talked about
with the various points at which there's energy

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:52.140
consumption looking ahead to what the techniques
are for carbon footprint aware software development.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:57.350
Something I find as a software developer and,
and doing profiling of things, one of the

00:09:57.350 --> 00:10:03.260
real big principles that you run into and,
and, what you run into in practice is knowing

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:06.970
what you're actually measuring.

00:10:06.970 --> 00:10:13.430
You know, if, if you're measuring you know
CPU cycle, or memory usage of something, it's

00:10:13.430 --> 00:10:18.000
when you do the measurement, are you actually
measuring the thing that you have that you

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:20.130
can make an impact on.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:28.630
So thinking about that what are your thoughts
about how, what metrics there would be to

00:10:28.630 --> 00:10:30.959
see that you're improving things?

00:10:30.959 --> 00:10:33.910
And then how do you actually do the measuring
on that.

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:35.959
Tejas: That's a great question.

00:10:35.959 --> 00:10:39.180
And that's actually what I aspire to do.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:43.499
As part of, you know, my job where today,
when you write code, you have unit tests that

00:10:43.499 --> 00:10:46.779
catch your bugs and you have integration tests.

00:10:46.779 --> 00:10:47.779
You deploy your code.

00:10:47.779 --> 00:10:51.400
There are a bunch of tests that run, and then
you know that whether it's good or not.

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:56.730
And I aspire to make a framework where, you
know, it's not just a functionally correct

00:10:56.730 --> 00:10:59.499
code, but environmentally good code.

00:10:59.499 --> 00:11:04.009
So you will have a framework or a series of
tests that you can run that, like you said,

00:11:04.009 --> 00:11:08.860
measure the memory usage, measure the CPU
cycles of your code, measure, the amount of

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:14.559
spin spinning of your like if you have spin
locks anywhere or detecting those kinds of

00:11:14.559 --> 00:11:19.490
things in your code and give you alternatives
for those also stuff what compilers do these

00:11:19.490 --> 00:11:20.639
days, right?

00:11:20.639 --> 00:11:23.689
Loop unrolling, some of these things that
you can do on your end.

00:11:23.689 --> 00:11:25.620
I think these would be good starting points.

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:30.029
And then you can use a lot of static analysis
tools that are already available in coding.

00:11:30.029 --> 00:11:34.850
So they would be a great starting point and
then like you said, you know, flame graphs

00:11:34.850 --> 00:11:37.529
are a good way to know which function is being
hit the most.

00:11:37.529 --> 00:11:41.760
And you can then get into that like which
particular function needs to be optimized

00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:47.259
the most for energy.

00:11:47.259 --> 00:11:48.259
So those are some of the tricks that you can
start off with.

00:11:48.259 --> 00:11:52.170
Bridget: So one of the things that has got
me interested in this topic is I, I did a

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:57.630
lot of research and thought a lot about changing
jobs or, you know, moving in a professional

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:03.770
direction to do something towards having some
impact on climate change, you know, not that

00:12:03.770 --> 00:12:06.860
I was going to figure out anything that solves
anything, but, you know, just try to move

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:09.999
over into some industry that that has more
of an impact.

00:12:09.999 --> 00:12:14.350
And in the course of doing that I found out
a lot and, and made some decisions based on

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:15.350
that.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:19.829
But I also talked to a lot of people, a lot
of software developers in my network who are

00:12:19.829 --> 00:12:21.160
interested in the same thing.

00:12:21.160 --> 00:12:24.949
I've heard this from so many people.

00:12:24.949 --> 00:12:31.050
How do you think taking a look at the impact
on climate change via the lens of carbon footprint

00:12:31.050 --> 00:12:36.339
aware software development how does that what
advice would you give somebody who's thinking

00:12:36.339 --> 00:12:42.379
about that would it be better to say go work
at Tesla and help them produce electric cars,

00:12:42.379 --> 00:12:47.699
which is, you know, it's direct impact and
climate change or using what you know about

00:12:47.699 --> 00:12:55.170
software development to make an impact on
how software and services impact on climate

00:12:55.170 --> 00:12:56.170
change.

00:12:56.170 --> 00:13:01.740
Like it put another way if I go work at Facebook
and I know these methods and I can reduce

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:08.670
their carbon footprint by 0.0, zero, whatever
1% given how, how big their service is versus

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:12.149
doing something that's direct, like electrical
cars or solar panels.

00:13:12.149 --> 00:13:15.339
What advice would you give somebody in, in
weighing those two options?

00:13:15.339 --> 00:13:16.619
Tejas: Sure, sure.

00:13:16.619 --> 00:13:20.579
And I, I truly believe that electric cars
are the future.

00:13:20.579 --> 00:13:25.720
If you look at the world of 40, 50 years from
now, what will the world look like?

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.329
I think it will be electric cars.

00:13:27.329 --> 00:13:30.290
It'll be cars that drive themselves, probably
not even cars.

00:13:30.290 --> 00:13:35.319
I don't even know what all will be there,
but I do believe that, you know at that scale

00:13:35.319 --> 00:13:40.350
it would make a lot of sense for anyone to,
you know, look for a career in building those.

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:44.610
So definitely that would be the first pick,
but a lot of us don't have options because

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:47.100
electric cars haven't reached a lot of countries
in the world.

00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:50.070
People do not have the technology to get there
yet.

00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:55.579
So do you, as an engineer, wait till that
comes over or do you try to move the needle?

00:13:55.579 --> 00:13:56.579
However, tiny it is.

00:13:56.579 --> 00:13:59.179
So, you know, do your own little bit in your
day to day work.

00:13:59.179 --> 00:14:00.670
And Facebook is a great example, right?

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:04.800
Like if you look at WhatsApp, the number of
people it touches is like 2 billion plus in

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:05.800
the world.

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:13.399
So if you look at 0.001% of that, it's still
a bigger number compared to let's say Tesla

00:14:13.399 --> 00:14:18.550
two years back where it was just still a tiny
fraction of the number of cars or emissions

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:19.670
that happen.

00:14:19.670 --> 00:14:24.410
I agree that with time we will come to a place
where we would, everyone would be working

00:14:24.410 --> 00:14:28.029
on something that is directly correlated to
the environment.

00:14:28.029 --> 00:14:32.949
So if I were sitting in California, I would
prefer Tesla going and making electric cars.

00:14:32.949 --> 00:14:36.470
But if I'm sitting in some remote corner of
the world where I don't have many options,

00:14:36.470 --> 00:14:41.199
I can still try to work at some other company
and write software something that I live and

00:14:41.199 --> 00:14:44.880
breathe in a way that, you know, can help
the environment or move the needle a little

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:45.880
bit.

00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:53.100
So I think no matter where you are, you can
always find a way to do the right thing.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:54.100
So, yeah.

